00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:17.660
So with that, let's talk
about virtual private cloud

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.680
reimagined.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.240
The reason why I'm
saying reimagined

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.950
is because we want to take a
look at what the virtual public

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.800
clouds traditional look like.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.950
What are some of the
hurdles that they may have?

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.980
And how we're proposing
to overcome them

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.540
with our architecture.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.160
We want to provide a way
to deploy efficiently,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.930
simple, flexibly.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:47.360
So how are we actually
proposing to do that?

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.030
First of all, our Google
Virtual Private Cloud is global.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.580
I want to focus
on the attributes

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:02.570
that we believe we can support
some of the simplification

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.730
and efficiency that
I mentioned before.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:09.200
So first of all, our Virtual
Private Cloud is global.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.770
That means that you can deploy
your workloads globally.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:17.720
And when you do so, you can
have automatic communication,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.880
private communication
across them,

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.627
across regions without
having to worry about putting

00:03:22.627 --> 00:03:24.710
in your own backbone,
having your own arrangements

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:26.300
about the networking
for communicating

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.440
those workloads across regions.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.680
And we will see that
that is true for cloud

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.020
to cloud communication
in Google.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.750
But its also true
when you are extending

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:42.530
the communication in a
hybrid environment with VPN

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.690
or Cloud Interconnect,
but also having

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:49.400
a global model in which a single
point of entry to our cloud

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.210
give you access to all your
global deployed workloads.

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:54.230
It is also shareable.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:58.730
And this is something that we
particularly feel proud of.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:02.150
Because what we
want to propose is

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:07.010
a model in which you have a
VPC for your organization.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:11.200
And this has been
recently launched in beta.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.645
Actually, as of yesterday.

00:04:13.645 --> 00:04:18.470
And it is a single share VPC
for your organization, which

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.950
means that you can
have developers

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:26.080
and applications, workloads,
that are isolated in projects.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.140
I'll talk a little bit more
about where the project is.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.690
But it provides a
multi-tenancy environment

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:35.900
with isolation of
those workloads

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:41.870
while they share a common
space for IP private networking

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:43.040
in which they collaborate.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.960
And then you can have a
central administration

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.800
for that space in terms
of security, IP location,

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.900
setting up routing, et cetera.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.510
The third attribute
is our Google

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:56.390
Virtual VPC is expandable.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:59.300
What that means is
that you can actually

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:07.070
increase the IP ranges of
your subnets in a lifetime.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:11.650
You don't need to shut
down any of your workloads.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.460
We complete the operation
without any service downtime.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.300
Therefore, you have
the flexibility,

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.570
as you grow your business
and your applications,

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:26.840
to expand that range without
having to worry upfront

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:30.770
of provisioning that IP space.

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:33.380
And then the last
part is private.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:36.230
We provide access-- and
again, this is something

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:40.580
that we're introducing
recently and, again,

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.550
we're announcing in beta.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:46.370
We're providing private
access to all our Google

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:50.000
managed services, that
is all our analytics,

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:57.200
Big Data, BigQuery,
storage, PubSub, et cetera,

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.000
privately from our VM instances.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.770
So let's take a little bit more.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:07.540
Let's take a look at some of
these attributes, what they

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.460
are in a little bit more depth.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.750
So our global VPC means that
you can connect your workloads,

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.140
again, privately.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:18.160
And then in a traditional
VPC, usually when

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.010
you want to connect
workloads across regions,

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:24.940
you need to somehow have some
means of private connectivity

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:29.890
and that could be through
a VPN or some type

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:35.530
of stitching mechanism across
those two different regions.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:41.200
With our Google
virtual VPC, we are

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.090
providing private communication
of those workloads

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.150
without really having to
prove anything at all.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:51.870
So that's presented to
you as a capability.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:53.720
And it doesn't even
need to be provisioned.

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.320
It's complete
transparent to the user.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:00.940
So in terms of what that
means in the bigger picture,

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:04.050
you're looking at the
locations that we have today.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:05.770
We're making a
lot of investments

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.050
in expanding geographically.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.750
So this picture
gives you a snapshot

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:13.900
of what we have today
in terms of regions.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.760
Those are the square boxes.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.220
And within those,
you see the number

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:21.725
of zones, availability
zones that are there.

00:07:21.725 --> 00:07:27.770
In blue, you have what
are the regions that

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:30.460
are going to be turned up
by the end of the year.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:35.854
And then I'm showing, as well,
the points of interconnect.

00:07:35.854 --> 00:07:37.270
Michael [INAUDIBLE]
in the morning

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:41.100
was talking about
how we're providing

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.410
Cloud Interconnect private.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:47.860
And those will be the points
in which for this year,

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:51.160
you will be able to
interconnect to get access

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:56.190
to our network and our regions
where Compute and Storage

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:57.350
handle the resources left.

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:03.680
Let's take a look
at the shared VPC.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:09.800
You will see it referred as
XPN when you go to our website.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:11.750
It's short for
Cross-Project Networking.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:15.710
But I think it's best
understood as a share virtual

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:18.210
private cloud for
your organization.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:20.480
What that means is
in a traditional VPC,

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.940
you usually have disjoint
private connectivity

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:26.120
across different accounts.

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:29.300
As well, if you have
different accounts,

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.910
you may need to have different
private connectivity.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:34.169
Because if you have
different VPCs,

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:36.350
one private connectivity
for each of those

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.780
VPCs for your
on-premises data center,

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:43.039
it's a decentralized model
for setting up policies.

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:47.450
And also, the developers need
to connect to that local VPC,

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:48.000
so to say.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.200
And so they need to be aware of
what the connectivity is there.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.390
What we're proposing
is a model in which

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:56.120
you have a single virtual
private connectivity

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:57.080
for your organization.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:01.040
And the organization is the
domain, just to be clear.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:05.310
So it maps to your
enterprise.com.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:08.300
That's where all the
projects are going to live.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:11.120
And with that
shared connectivity,

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:14.660
what you have as well is a
single private interconnect

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:17.000
to your on-premises
will be made available

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:20.270
as well to all the
accounts, projects that are

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.160
connected to that shared VPC.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:23.780
And then it is a
centralized model

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:27.800
for setting up
security and routing

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:29.900
across your organization.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.980
And the developers will see
that [INAUDIBLE] plug and play

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:33.810
or as a service.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:38.030
And they don't need to be
aware of the routing mechanisms

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:40.400
or the connectivity
policy associated to that.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:43.820
They'll just see it as a domain
to which they connect when

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:46.860
they spin up their services.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:51.350
So let's take a look at what
it means really in terms

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:52.910
of what do you need to set up.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:55.700
So there will be an
organization MY-ORG.com.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:59.660
There's going to be a security
or a network administrator.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:01.640
This can be actually
two personas.

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:04.970
You can have those
roles separated.

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:09.090
But they're going
to be responsible

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:12.560
of the administration of that
share connectivity domain.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.570
That is the private
connectivity domain.

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:20.570
They are going to be
responsible of the AP allocation

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.610
for the organization.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:25.550
And then they're going to
be setting up firewalls

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:28.020
for that shared VPC.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:31.730
And you can actually connect
that single share VPC

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:35.120
to your customer data center.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:38.060
And it's made available
to the organization.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:43.940
Now, when a web server developer
starts its own project,

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:45.650
it will just spin it up.

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:48.260
It will directly
connect to that subnet.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:52.100
And then it will get access
as well to on-premises,

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:54.860
the same for a new-- this
is just imaginary services.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.950
You have a
recommendation service

00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:00.110
that is used by the web server.

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:01.080
You spin it up.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:03.440
You're able to communicate
in the same space.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.570
And the same for
the database DevOps.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.870
And the key is all those
different applications

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:12.140
are owned by
different people that

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:14.580
are owning their own projects.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:17.540
So the administration
and ownership of those

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:20.562
is very clear.

00:11:20.562 --> 00:11:21.770
There's a demarcation domain.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.040
So let's take an example.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.660
And this goes back to what
we were showing before.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:30.840
A web server that has
a couple of services

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:32.310
for recommendation, analytics.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:35.340
And then we'll
fill in information

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:40.320
into a cloud BigQuery as
analytics, Big Data example.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:43.620
And then there is an application
that is living in on-premises.

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:47.110
And then those services, they
need to communicate with it.

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:49.820
Let's say, I know, an LDAP
directory or anything that it

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:52.560
hasn't been able
to move to cloud

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:56.820
or is difficult to re-platform,
it's not worth it, et cetera.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:01.970
So usually in a traditional VPC,
you have different projects.

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:05.850
So products in
Google Cloud, they

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:09.630
are containers in which
you apply policies

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:11.400
like quota, billing.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:14.370
So it is really a
demarcation mechanism

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:16.780
for [INAUDIBLE] as well.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.720
So it is a demarcation
mechanism for ownership

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:24.000
on a lot of these
policies out in force.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:29.940
So each of these owners will
spin up their own services

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:31.410
independently.

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:34.230
And they will have to
create their own VPCs

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:37.485
and then set up
security firewalls.

00:12:37.485 --> 00:12:39.570
If they need to
collaborate, they

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:42.290
should set up some type of
internal connection mechanism,

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:45.100
VPC peering, or
something similar,

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:49.765
and then set up connectivity
for on-premises.

00:12:49.765 --> 00:12:53.880
And if you want to talk
to external services,

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:59.040
then you will have public
IPs in order to do so.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:00.520
Now let's take a step back.

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:03.510
What we're trying to do, we want
these services to collaborate.

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:05.550
We want all of
them to get access

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:08.460
to some application
on on-premises.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:13.050
And we want to get access to a
Google managed service, which

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:16.570
is also hosted in
our infrastructure.

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:20.000
So how can we do this better?

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:24.250
We can have these services
create and then spin up

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:28.360
their own projects,
so that we really

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:30.040
can keep separate billing.

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:33.160
We can enforce quotas for them.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:35.260
They can be owners of
any of the resources

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:38.200
that are spin up there.

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:40.360
But when it comes
to the connectivity,

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:46.030
you can have a shared VPC that
is set up across all of them.

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:48.220
And then set up
the firewalls that

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:52.500
will determine how they will
talk externally and internally.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:56.170
And set up a single
private connectivity

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:58.600
to your on-premises.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:02.650
And if you're talking with
a Google managed service,

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:04.480
that communication is private.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:09.400
So you don't need public IPs
in order to achieve that.

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:14.870
With that, I want to welcome you
to our vision of Google Virtual

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:19.880
Private Cloud for global,
simple, flexible hybrid

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:20.870
deployments.

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:23.507
I welcome Neha
Pattan to the stage.

00:14:23.507 --> 00:14:25.090
And she's going to
show us some demos.

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:33.138
