00:14:58.340 --> 00:15:02.030
and pretty efficient.

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:05.080
So let's start with a
fictitious company, Dance Moves,

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:09.210
Incorporated, of which you
are a principal engineer.

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:11.160
Let's imagine that the
CTO of your company

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:13.410
comes up with an
interesting task for you.

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:16.500
And your task is to design
and build a web application

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:20.280
server that serves dancing
lessons to subscribers

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:22.720
around the world.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:24.220
You're handed a
list of requirements

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:26.053
to make sure that the
service that you build

00:15:26.053 --> 00:15:29.170
is highly available and
can scale seamlessly

00:15:29.170 --> 00:15:33.850
to growth in usage and demand.

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:36.220
For ease of development,
deployment, and speed

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:38.140
of execution, you
would like to make sure

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:40.904
that the service that you
build can run in the cloud

00:15:40.904 --> 00:15:42.445
and it is backed by
virtual machines.

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:47.230
Now, let's imagine
that your company

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:50.680
is running an Active Directory
server in a data center

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:52.240
on-premises.

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:54.400
And in order to connect
the virtual network running

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:56.830
in the cloud to this
Active Directory server,

00:15:56.830 --> 00:15:58.930
you would like to have
a secure connection

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:02.240
between your virtual
network and the on-prem.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:06.850
Now, as you finish
coding your application

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:10.780
and you begin to deploy
it, you anticipate traffic

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:13.540
from certain parts
of North America.

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:15.040
In order to serve
their traffic, you

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:19.420
create two VPCs, one in US
West and the other in US East.

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:21.520
And you begin to
deploy your application

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:24.245
to virtual machines that are
provisioned at these VPCs.

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:30.130
You create a VPN connection
to securely connect

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.186
to your on-prem.

00:16:32.186 --> 00:16:34.590
Now, in a traditional
VPC model, you

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:37.510
would then connect
your regional VPCs

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:41.110
to each other through VPN
or through VPC peering

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:43.879
or through public IPs.

00:16:43.879 --> 00:16:45.420
And the reason for
this is that there

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:48.120
is no private connectivity
between regional VPCs,

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:53.800
in a traditional
model, by default.

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:56.110
Since Google's VPC
is global in nature,

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:58.600
all the VMs that you create
in your virtual network

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:01.120
in any region globally
get private connectivity

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.910
to each other by default,
and also get connectivity

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:07.510
to your on-prem
through a single VPN.

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:09.550
Although for throughput
and performance reasons,

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:12.580
you may create one
VPN in each region,

00:17:12.580 --> 00:17:16.480
Google's VPC allows you to
create multiple VPNs attached

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:19.089
to the same virtual network.

00:17:19.089 --> 00:17:22.750
With Google VPC, which runs on
Google's own backbone network

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:26.700
infrastructure, which
serves our Google services,

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:29.890
you are ensured that you
get high quality of service

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:32.260
and low congestion.

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:34.210
And you are ensured
that you do not

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:37.315
need to create VPN
connections or use public IPs

00:17:37.315 --> 00:17:40.840
or use VPC peering in
order to get subnetworks

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:43.617
in multiple regions globally
to have private connectivity

00:17:43.617 --> 00:17:44.200
to each other.

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:50.640
Now, as your application
grows and as the users

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:53.430
of your application grows,
you begin to notice traffic

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:55.360
from other regions as well.

00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:57.750
So let's imagine that you
begin to notice that you're

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:00.090
seeing traffic from Europe.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:03.460
How do you deal with this?

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:07.950
Now, in a traditional VPC
model, the overhead and the cost

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:10.380
associated with your
deployment increases

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:15.570
with every new region that must
be added to your deployment.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:17.640
Google VPC, being
global in nature,

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:20.490
you're able to scale your
application seamlessly,

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:26.370
horizontally by adding new
regions with minimal overhead.

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:29.340
Now, let's imagine that you had
provisioned your subnetworks

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:31.460
to be slash 26's.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:34.380
Now, a slash 26
gives you 64 IPs.

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:38.714
GCE will reserve
four of those IPs.

00:18:38.714 --> 00:18:41.130
So let's imagine that you had
provisioned your subnetworks

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:46.000
to be able to create 60 VMs
in each of these subnetworks.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:47.730
Now, as your application
grows and you

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:50.840
begin to notice more
traffic in each region,

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:55.040
you begin to notice that you
are running out of IP space.

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:56.710
This is not a problem.

00:18:56.710 --> 00:19:01.360
With Google VPC, you can expand
the subnet ranges seamlessly

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:03.250
without affecting any
of the traffic that

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:06.460
is served by the existing VMs
and also without affecting

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:09.760
any of the IPs that are
owned by the existing VMs.

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:11.980
So you get to change
the ranges associated

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:17.580
with your regional networks
with zero downtime whatsoever.

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:20.330
Let's take a quick
look at a demonstration

00:19:20.330 --> 00:19:28.480
of how to create global
virtual networks and how to--

00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:36.330
can we switch to a demo, please?

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:40.385
So here you can see that in
my Dance Moves web application

00:19:40.385 --> 00:19:43.200
server, I have a single
network right now,

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:45.000
which is a production network.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:48.520
Let's say I would like
to create a new network.

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:49.760
I can give it a name.

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:52.000
Let's say, the test network.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:54.056
And I can give it a description.

00:19:57.630 --> 00:20:01.806
Now, when creating networks,
you are given two options.

00:20:01.806 --> 00:20:03.430
One is that you can
create an auto mode

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:06.730
network in which one subnetwork
is created automatically

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:10.150
for you of a fixed size.

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:12.490
And the size is a slash 20.

00:20:12.490 --> 00:20:16.300
So every region will get
a slash 20 by default.

00:20:16.300 --> 00:20:19.020
When GCP adds new
regions, then we

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:20.680
will make sure that
a new subnetwork

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:26.200
is added in every auto
mode network by default.

00:20:26.200 --> 00:20:30.320
The other mode is
custom mode, where

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:31.790
you can create as
many subnetworks

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:33.289
as you desire in
the network and you

00:20:33.289 --> 00:20:37.170
can create them with any
size that you desire as well.

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:46.650
So let's say that I create a
test subnetwork in US West 1.

00:20:46.650 --> 00:20:53.760
So here I can choose a region
and give it an IP range.

00:20:57.960 --> 00:20:59.760
And that's pretty much it.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:03.470
That's all it took for me
to set up a network with one

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:05.570
subnetwork in a single region.

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:08.960
Now, let's imagine that in
the production network, as you

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:10.950
can see, I have four subnets.

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:14.410
They're all slash 16's except
for what I have as a share

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:17.010
subnet 4, which is a slash 24.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:20.560
So if I would like to
expand the subnetwork,

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:22.460
I just go ahead and edit it.

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:27.555
And I can change the
[INAUDIBLE] range to slash 16.

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:36.770
And that's pretty much it.

00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:40.130
So basically, with a few clicks,
I was able to create a network.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:43.910
And I was also able to expand
the IP [INAUDIBLE] range

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:47.460
of a single subnetwork
in my network.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:49.580
So here you can see the
test network is created

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:55.090
and the subnetwork in US West
1, the 10.40 is now increased

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:57.740
to as slash 16.

00:21:57.740 --> 00:22:00.413
Can we switch back
to the slides?

00:22:00.413 --> 00:22:01.379
Thank you.

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:07.610
Now, as your application
becomes more popular,

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:10.640
you begin to see growth
not only in usage, but also

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:15.310
in the demand for the features
that your service supports.

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:18.280
With Google shared VPC,
which Ines described,

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:21.750
we also call that XPN, short
for Cross-Project Networking,

00:22:21.750 --> 00:22:23.760
you are now able to
leverage the services that

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.910
are built in-house by other
teams in your organization

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:29.190
with minimal overhead.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.590
You're also able to adopt
Google managed services,

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:35.580
like the machine learning
services, our data warehousing

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:39.930
services, and our storage
APIs, like BigQuery and MySQL.

00:22:39.930 --> 00:22:42.420
You're able to access
these privately

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:45.030
through private connectivity,
so that you do not

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:47.190
have to assign public
IPs to your VMs

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:49.620
in order to access
these Google services.

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:55.544
So let's imagine that to the
web application server that you

00:22:55.544 --> 00:22:56.960
built and deployed
previously, you

00:22:56.960 --> 00:22:59.180
would like to enhance
that by adding

00:22:59.180 --> 00:23:00.860
the functionality
of recommendation,

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:03.860
personalization, and
analytics, which are services

00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:05.780
that are built by
other development

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:08.370
teams in your organization.

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:11.820
Now, with shared
VPC, also called XPN,

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:15.830
you will now be able to enable
multiple development teams

00:23:15.830 --> 00:23:19.580
in your organization to work
and cooperate and collaborate

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:24.620
effectively and collaborate
in an autonomous manner

00:23:24.620 --> 00:23:26.410
by providing a single
[INAUDIBLE] shared

00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:28.170
virtual network.

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:30.260
So you can see the
recommendation service,

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.600
the personalization service, and
the analytics services are all

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:34.950
service projects.

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:38.340
And the development teams
from these service projects

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:41.340
can create virtual machines and
provision their applications

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:43.110
on those virtual
machines without having

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:45.360
to worry about network
administration, which

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:49.140
is done centrally by the
network administrator associated

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:53.790
with the shared VPC network.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:57.120
The network administrator can
also handle VPN connections

00:23:57.120 --> 00:23:59.850
to on-prem, so that every
development team does not

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:04.040
have to create a
separate VPN connection.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:07.390
And since the
developers have autonomy

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:09.850
in managing their
services, they can

00:24:09.850 --> 00:24:13.810
control the IM rules that are
configured on their projects.

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:16.540
So they can configure
fine-grained IM.

00:24:16.540 --> 00:24:19.270
And they also have control
over billing and quota

00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:20.440
and running their services.

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:26.230
The centralized
security administrator

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:30.490
can configure security
policies in your organization

00:24:30.490 --> 00:24:33.970
by creating firewall rules
to control which subnetworks

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:35.870
have access to the internet.

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:38.630
So here you can see the web
application server has access

00:24:38.630 --> 00:24:41.080
to the internet versus
which subnetworks

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:43.010
have only private connectivity.

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:49.326
So they can only be
accessed over RFC 1980 IPs.

00:24:49.326 --> 00:24:53.300
And IM can also be used
to control and to restrict

00:24:53.300 --> 00:24:57.320
the permissions for attaching
public IPs to virtual machines

00:24:57.320 --> 00:25:02.420
by specifying the IM permissions
on the subnetworks that

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:04.700
are created that are
used for creating

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:07.730
the virtual machines in them.

00:25:07.730 --> 00:25:10.310
So how would we configure
this for the application

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:12.800
that we have built?

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:15.980
Now, Google VPC allows you to
create multiple subnetworks

00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:17.030
per region.

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:20.840
So here we are considering
a single region, US West,

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:23.200
where we have created
four subnetworks of size

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:25.010
slash 16 each.

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:28.334
So you can see the 10.10
slash 16 subnetwork has access

00:25:28.334 --> 00:25:29.000
to the internet.

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:31.070
It is the internet-facing
subnetwork,

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:35.934
which will be responding
to requests from users.

00:25:35.934 --> 00:25:37.850
And then you have the
other three subnetworks,

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:40.790
which have private connectivity
and are accessed only

00:25:40.790 --> 00:25:42.870
internally.

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:47.010
With shared VPC or XPN, you
can configure every subnetwork

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:50.280
to be accessible by one
or more service projects

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:53.620
to create virtual machines
attached to those subnetworks.

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:58.050
So here you can see how the
10.20 slash 16 is configured

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:01.740
to have the recommendation
project access that subnetwork

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:03.840
to attach VMs in it.

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:06.120
And similarly, for the
personalization and analytics

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:12.080
projects, they can access the
10.30 slash 16 and the 10.40

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:14.780
slash 16 subnetworks.

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:16.712
Now, the development
teams in these service

00:26:16.712 --> 00:26:18.670
projects-- the recommendation,
personalization,

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:22.135
and the analytics projects--
can provision VMs and deploy

00:26:22.135 --> 00:26:23.510
their applications
without having

00:26:23.510 --> 00:26:27.260
to worry about the IP
range management or the VPN

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:29.720
connection management,
which is done by the network

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:33.920
administrator, or having to
worry about the firewalls

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:37.130
by the security administrator.

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:39.830
Similarly, if certain
service projects

00:26:39.830 --> 00:26:42.980
which require private
access to Google APIs,

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:47.120
then the network administrator
can configure this

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:49.905
on the subnetwork that
is used by the service.

00:26:49.905 --> 00:26:52.280
So in this case, the network
administrator would consider

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:58.290
the 10.20 slash 16 subnetwork to
have private access to Google.

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:02.240
Let's take a look at how easy
it is to set up XPN and enable

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:03.405
private access to Google.

00:27:06.137 --> 00:27:07.595
Can we switch to
demo mode, please?

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:13.980
So here you can see I've
selected the Dance Moves web

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:15.720
application server.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:18.650
And I'm about to set up XPN.

00:27:18.650 --> 00:27:20.490
This asks me
whether I would like

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:25.700
to ensure that the XPN host
project is the Dance Moves web

00:27:25.700 --> 00:27:27.000
app server.

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:29.460
And I will confirm that.

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:30.880
Now, we're given two options.

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:32.820
One is that you
can either choose

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:36.870
to share all the subnetworks
in the chosen host project

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:39.210
or you can choose
individual subnetworks

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:41.580
to share with the
service projects.

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:46.990
So let's choose to share
individual subnetworks.

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:52.280
So since the 10.20 slash 16 is
shared with the recommendation

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:56.760
project, I'm going to go
ahead and configure that.

00:27:56.760 --> 00:28:00.210
So here you can see that
the recommendation project

00:28:00.210 --> 00:28:03.900
has no access to the
production network.

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:10.870
And it basically has access
to the shared subnet2,

00:28:10.870 --> 00:28:14.110
which is of IP range
10.20 slash 16.

00:28:14.110 --> 00:28:16.160
And the project
it is shared with

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:17.410
is the recommendation project.

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:20.272
So it's as simple as that.

00:28:20.272 --> 00:28:21.980
Now we can go back to
the Attach Projects

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:23.260
and attach more projects.

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:27.960
So let's say the personalization
project gets the 10.30 slash 16

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:29.720
subnet.

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:36.330
And sure enough, the analytics
service will get the 10.40

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.777
slash 16.

00:28:38.777 --> 00:28:39.860
And that's pretty much it.

00:28:39.860 --> 00:28:44.160
So at this point, I
have three subnetworks

00:28:44.160 --> 00:28:47.070
that I've shared with
one service project each.

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:49.860
And I can view this here.

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:54.960
And I can basically look at all
the IP ranges that I've shared.

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:57.270
And also look at
the fine-grained IM

00:28:57.270 --> 00:29:02.070
that I can then modify in
order to suit the correct IM

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:05.480
requirements in my organization.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:09.850
So next, let's see how to
enable private IP access.

00:29:09.850 --> 00:29:14.710
So as we saw, we would like to
configure the recommendation

00:29:14.710 --> 00:29:17.340
project to have
access to Google.

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:19.750
And that would be
pretty much as simple

00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:26.360
as toggling this, where
I edit the subnetwork

00:29:26.360 --> 00:29:29.810
and I configure it to have
private Google access.

00:29:32.447 --> 00:29:33.530
And that's pretty much it.

00:29:33.530 --> 00:29:34.946
So basically, with
a single click,

00:29:34.946 --> 00:29:40.240
I was able to configure private
access to Google services.

00:29:40.240 --> 00:29:43.520
Can we switch back
to the slides?

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:46.880
Thank you.

00:29:46.880 --> 00:29:48.404
Now, as your
application grows, you

00:29:48.404 --> 00:29:50.570
will find the need to have
the virtual machines that

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:54.159
are serving your web application
traffic to be serving traffic

00:29:54.159 --> 00:29:55.200
behind the load balancer.

00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:58.490
And you can use Google's
global HTTP/HTTPS load

00:29:58.490 --> 00:30:01.840
balancing for this purpose.

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:04.400
With shared VPC, you
can now configure

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:06.980
these virtual machines to
have private connectivity

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:08.630
to the recommendation,
personalization,

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:12.320
and analytics services, where
the virtual machines serving

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:14.810
traffic in those services
are serving traffic

00:30:14.810 --> 00:30:17.650
behind an internal
load balancer.

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:19.790
We recently announced
the general availability

00:30:19.790 --> 00:30:21.230
of internal load balancing.

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:23.900
And are now happy to
announce that this can also

00:30:23.900 --> 00:30:26.170
be configured in a
shared VPC model.

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:34.640
So to recap what we saw,
Google VPC is global in nature.

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:37.640
You can enable your workloads
in any Google Cloud region

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:40.850
and get private connectivity
between all the cloud regions

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:44.300
without having to take
any further action.

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:48.670
Google's global network is
provided to you as a service.

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:52.250
You also get [INAUDIBLE] access
to Google managed services.

00:30:52.250 --> 00:30:54.092
And this is a very
rich feature set,

00:30:54.092 --> 00:30:55.800
as you might have seen
during the keynote

00:30:55.800 --> 00:30:58.010
and during the other
breakout sessions.

00:30:58.010 --> 00:30:59.810
The machine learning
APIs, we have

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:04.240
[INAUDIBLE] warehousing APIs,
and we have our pub serve API,

00:31:04.240 --> 00:31:06.830
we have storage APIs, and
we have a lot many more

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:08.120
coming up as well.

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:13.740
The second point is that Google
VPC is shareable in nature.

00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:16.710
You can now enable your
organization to work in a way

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:20.160
that you have multiple projects
and multiple teams collaborate

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:24.360
together by having the
development teams focus on what

00:31:24.360 --> 00:31:26.940
they do best, which is
to develop the business

00:31:26.940 --> 00:31:29.760
logic for their applications
without having to worry

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:33.090
about network and security
administration, which is done

00:31:33.090 --> 00:31:35.460
centrally by the
network and security

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:39.900
administrators of
your organization.

00:31:39.900 --> 00:31:43.150
And the third point is that
Google VPC is expandable.

00:31:43.150 --> 00:31:46.610
Learning is great, but you
shouldn't always have to.

00:31:46.610 --> 00:31:49.670
If you notice that your
application is growing

00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:52.610
and you did not plan
for this growth upfront,

00:31:52.610 --> 00:31:56.240
then you can actually
expand [INAUDIBLE] ranges

00:31:56.240 --> 00:31:58.520
that are associated with
your virtual network,

00:31:58.520 --> 00:32:00.500
without having any
downtime, without affecting

00:32:00.500 --> 00:32:02.570
any of the other
networking resources

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:05.930
in your virtual network.

00:32:05.930 --> 00:32:08.180
This is a list of
some of the talks

00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:12.080
that you will find interesting
that also focus on Google Cloud

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:13.640
networking.

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:15.680
If you might have missed
some of these talks,

00:32:15.680 --> 00:32:17.455
then the videos will
be available shortly.

00:32:17.455 --> 00:32:22.650
And please make
sure to catch those.

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:26.110
With that, I would like to
invite Stefan from Spotify.

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:29.350
And he's going to share some
of Spotify's experiences

00:32:29.350 --> 00:32:31.690
with integrating with GCP.

00:32:31.690 --> 00:32:34.364
Thanks, everyone.

00:32:34.364 --> 00:32:38.917
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:38.917 --> 00:32:39.750
STEFAN LINDBLOM: OK.

00:32:39.750 --> 00:32:42.040
So hello, everyone.

00:32:42.040 --> 00:32:43.080
My name is Stefan.

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:45.870
I'm a network
engineer at Spotify.

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:47.610
I'm working in a
group of six people.

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:49.940
And based in
Stockholm in Sweden.

00:32:49.940 --> 00:32:52.500
And I've been there
since late 2011,

00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:55.650
so that's about
five and 1/2 years.

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:59.490
And when I started, we were
around 300 employees globally.

00:32:59.490 --> 00:33:01.770
And we're now over 2,000.

00:33:01.770 --> 00:33:03.720
So we've grown a
lot very quickly.

00:33:03.720 --> 00:33:07.460
And the same goes for the
infrastructure part as well.

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:10.595
So I'm going to talk about how
we're actually moving into GCP

00:33:10.595 --> 00:33:16.540
and how we're combining
on-premise and the cloud.

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:18.930
So first of all,
what is Spotify?

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:21.330
Spotify is a music
streaming service

00:33:21.330 --> 00:33:24.570
that allows all users to
play their favorite music

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:28.410
and build playlists and share
them with family or friends.

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:33.110
And one big part of this
is discovering new music.

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:36.990
Yeah, so some numbers
of the service.

00:33:36.990 --> 00:33:40.890
We have around 100 million
subscribers or users.

00:33:40.890 --> 00:33:42.420
Sorry.

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:44.580
And about 50
million are actually

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:46.790
paying for the service.

00:33:46.790 --> 00:33:49.860
Around 40 million songs and
over two billion playlists.

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:53.750
And we're available
in 60 markets today.

00:33:56.460 --> 00:33:59.160
So how is Spotify actually
built or the infrastructure

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:00.280
behind it?

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:03.870
So we have four data
centers, where it's actually

00:34:03.870 --> 00:34:05.670
co-location facilities.

00:34:05.670 --> 00:34:08.410
So we don't own the
actual space ourselves.

00:34:08.410 --> 00:34:13.590
One in London, one in Stockholm,
and then San Jose, and Ashburn.

00:34:13.590 --> 00:34:17.159
So spread out over this, we
have around 12,000 servers,

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:20.909
which is just normal
commodity hardware.

00:34:20.909 --> 00:34:23.909
That the network
team, six people,

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:27.150
we're responsible for delivering
internet access and access

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:29.400
across the data
centers themselves

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:33.489
and then connectivity into GCP.

00:34:33.489 --> 00:34:37.500
So how we built this is we
have lots of small squads

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:42.739
at Spotify, which are operating
over 600 microservices.

00:34:42.739 --> 00:34:47.560
And each squad may have five
to 10 services that they own.

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:50.610
And they're operational
responsible for those as well.

00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:53.750
So we don't have a big
operational [INAUDIBLE] take

00:34:53.750 --> 00:34:56.790
on call, for example, for
all of the small services.

00:34:56.790 --> 00:35:00.260
And some of them is the
search system, playlist,

00:35:00.260 --> 00:35:03.740
or the user login system.

00:35:03.740 --> 00:35:05.870
But we also have some
pretty large services,

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:07.640
like the Hadoop
cluster, which is

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:09.770
one of the biggest in Europe.

00:35:09.770 --> 00:35:12.890
And we have some big
storage clusters as well

00:35:12.890 --> 00:35:15.380
that span our data
centers, which

00:35:15.380 --> 00:35:16.970
are relying pretty
heavily on our site

00:35:16.970 --> 00:35:18.930
to site connectivity,
which is something

00:35:18.930 --> 00:35:22.190
I will get back to you about.

00:35:22.190 --> 00:35:24.890
So why are we
moving to the cloud?

00:35:24.890 --> 00:35:28.070
So back when we
started in 2006, we

00:35:28.070 --> 00:35:30.950
had the production servers
in the closet in the office,

00:35:30.950 --> 00:35:31.835
pretty much.

00:35:31.835 --> 00:35:35.570
And it was pretty easy to
just go and buy more hardware,

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:39.020
and get a rack or
two and then 40,

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:41.770
and just drive
the servers there.

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:46.790
But scaling your own hardware
and keeping up and having

00:35:46.790 --> 00:35:48.800
people to manage
all of the hardware

00:35:48.800 --> 00:35:52.010
takes, yeah, both
people and time.

00:35:52.010 --> 00:35:55.910
And our core business is not
really to operate data centers,

00:35:55.910 --> 00:35:58.160
but it's to actually
build the best music

00:35:58.160 --> 00:36:00.150
service that's out there.

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:03.840
So that's what we
want to focus on.

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:06.512
And this is a quote from
my colleague Jyrki--

00:36:06.512 --> 00:36:09.110
"If you can't beat
them, join them."

00:36:09.110 --> 00:36:12.690
What it means is that Spotify
could probably never outrun

00:36:12.690 --> 00:36:15.950
Google in terms of
physical hardware,

00:36:15.950 --> 00:36:19.120
so let's join them instead
and use their hardware.

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:23.790
And why Google Cloud?

00:36:23.790 --> 00:36:26.400
So Google has a
lot of experience

00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:28.990
with Hadoop and
MapReduce, for example.

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:32.070
And that's something that
Spotify is using a lot,

00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:35.310
or the big data processing.

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:40.560
So we can benefit a lot from
Google's experience of that.

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:43.980
For example, BigQuery
jobs are actually

00:36:43.980 --> 00:36:47.100
running in a few
seconds compared to ours

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:51.010
in our on-premise, which
is a big improvement.

00:36:51.010 --> 00:36:52.950
And when we started
working with Google,

00:36:52.950 --> 00:36:57.030
we noticed a good cultural
much, like open source,

00:36:57.030 --> 00:37:01.710
and we're able to innovate
on new projects and so on.

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:07.380
And we feel like Google
is listening to our ideas

00:37:07.380 --> 00:37:11.150
and, yeah, all of that.

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:14.100
And we actually
noticed a lower latency

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:17.850
compared to serving users
from our own data centers

00:37:17.850 --> 00:37:19.700
since we moved into GCP.

00:37:19.700 --> 00:37:21.660
And that's pretty
important to us,

00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:23.600
because every
millisecond matters

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:25.970
from when you press Play till
you get the music playing.

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:32.820
And some of the benefits of
having a shared VPC or XPN

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:35.440
and how Spotify is using it.

00:37:35.440 --> 00:37:39.680
So each microservice at Spotify
can get its own project today

00:37:39.680 --> 00:37:41.514
in Google Cloud.

00:37:41.514 --> 00:37:47.000
And before we had XPN, we had
to have separate networking

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:49.540
and separate firewall
policies, for example,

00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:51.510
for all of the projects.

00:37:51.510 --> 00:37:55.030
And that's a big task
to maintain, especially

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:56.756
for a single network team.

00:37:56.756 --> 00:38:02.170
So today we can give each
microservice its own project

00:38:02.170 --> 00:38:05.050
and they can all connect back
to the shared network that we

00:38:05.050 --> 00:38:08.190
have, which provides
VPN tunneled access back

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:13.450
to our on-premise and security
policies or firewall policies.

00:38:13.450 --> 00:38:16.780
So the developers of a
project or a microservice,

00:38:16.780 --> 00:38:19.990
they can manage pretty much
all the settings on their own.

00:38:19.990 --> 00:38:22.420
But they can't,
for example, change

00:38:22.420 --> 00:38:24.760
the whole global networking
settings or the security

00:38:24.760 --> 00:38:26.500
policies.

00:38:26.500 --> 00:38:30.930
So yeah, everything can't
go down all of a sudden.

00:38:30.930 --> 00:38:34.380
And right now, we have more
than 250 projects in GCP.

00:38:34.380 --> 00:38:37.030
And more than 800 developers
are working actively

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:41.160
on the GCP platform.

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:44.300
And let's get technical.

00:38:44.300 --> 00:38:48.230
So the Spotify
site-to-site VPN, this

00:38:48.230 --> 00:38:50.410
is something that we've
developed over the years.

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:54.560
And it's what we use for
communicating between our data

00:38:54.560 --> 00:38:56.840
centers or on-premise.

00:38:56.840 --> 00:38:58.250
So in each corner,
you can see we

00:38:58.250 --> 00:39:01.770
have one of the data
centers represented.

00:39:01.770 --> 00:39:04.400
And between all of
this, so we just

00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:08.090
buy internet transit
for each data center.

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:12.280
And then we put a few VPN
gateways in each site.

00:39:12.280 --> 00:39:16.140
And we have lots of IPSec
tunnels going between them.

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:20.120
So how we do that is the
total amount of tunnels

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:22.790
is actually 192 IPSec tunnels.

00:39:22.790 --> 00:39:26.580
And on top of each tunnel,
there's a BGP session.

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:32.390
So this gives us quite a lot of
ECMP paths or load balancing.

00:39:32.390 --> 00:39:34.940
So if we were to look at, for
example London to Stockholm,

00:39:34.940 --> 00:39:39.210
you would see around 42 paths
that you can use at that time.

00:39:39.210 --> 00:39:41.210
And the reason for having
this amount of tunnels

00:39:41.210 --> 00:39:45.390
is that we can't really control
the internet and congestion.

00:39:45.390 --> 00:39:49.650
So if we split all the
traffic up into smaller flows,

00:39:49.650 --> 00:39:53.120
we have a greater chance of
not hitting any bottlenecks

00:39:53.120 --> 00:39:54.840
on the internet.

00:39:54.840 --> 00:39:58.880
So we have a few years of
experience with a pretty large

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:00.785
IPSec setup.

00:40:00.785 --> 00:40:02.330
And that's something
if we wanted

00:40:02.330 --> 00:40:05.770
to keep as we're
migrating to GCP.

00:40:08.380 --> 00:40:12.730
So connecting to GCP from
our on-premise data centers.

00:40:12.730 --> 00:40:16.270
First of all, IPSec over
the internet, that's

00:40:16.270 --> 00:40:21.130
how we started when we connected
to Google in the first place.

00:40:21.130 --> 00:40:25.735
And that's fine for evaluating
and proof of concept.

00:40:25.735 --> 00:40:29.410
But yeah, we also noticed
that in the beginning,

00:40:29.410 --> 00:40:32.880
the IPSec performance
was not what

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:35.470
we were used to between
our own data centers,

00:40:35.470 --> 00:40:36.970
because we're talking
about hundreds

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:39.980
and hundreds of servers
talking to each other.

00:40:39.980 --> 00:40:43.570
But that improved
very rapidly in GCP.

00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:47.050
And today we're just treating
it as another data center,

00:40:47.050 --> 00:40:49.042
pretty much.

00:40:49.042 --> 00:40:53.830
And yeah, in the beginning,
we had static routing

00:40:53.830 --> 00:40:56.150
between our data
centers and GCP.

00:40:56.150 --> 00:40:59.620
But as the cloud router
has been coming out,

00:40:59.620 --> 00:41:01.240
we actually have
dynamic routing.

00:41:01.240 --> 00:41:04.570
And this is BGP right now.

00:41:04.570 --> 00:41:11.094
So we have a pretty big
IPSec and BGP setup into GCP.

00:41:11.094 --> 00:41:14.990
And as bandwidth requirements
grew between us and GCP,

00:41:14.990 --> 00:41:17.360
as we moved more
and more services,

00:41:17.360 --> 00:41:20.630
we started doing the
Google Cloud Interconnect.

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:26.940
So we're connecting in multiple
common locations with Google

00:41:26.940 --> 00:41:30.680
where you typically have peering
with any other vendor as well.

00:41:33.910 --> 00:41:38.220
So Spotify VPN to
GCP via peering.

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:40.570
So let's start from
the left-hand side

00:41:40.570 --> 00:41:43.870
here on the diagram.

00:41:43.870 --> 00:41:45.580
We have our Spotify
office, for example.

00:41:45.580 --> 00:41:47.830
We connect all of the offices
back to our data centers

00:41:47.830 --> 00:41:51.830
with IPSec and BGP as well.

00:41:51.830 --> 00:41:56.050
So imagine that you have some
traffic going from the data

00:41:56.050 --> 00:41:58.810
center into Google
Cloud Storage,

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:01.790
this would just go over the
peering links that we have.

00:42:01.790 --> 00:42:03.520
But it wouldn't go over IPSec.

00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:07.760
It would be encrypted with HTTPS
or TLS on the application layer

00:42:07.760 --> 00:42:08.950
instead.

00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:12.040
But if we have a server
in the data center talking

00:42:12.040 --> 00:42:14.745
to a Compute Engine
VM in the cloud,

00:42:14.745 --> 00:42:18.650
then they could actually be
encrypted with IPSec instead.

00:42:18.650 --> 00:42:22.100
So how that's working is it's
going over our VPN gateways

00:42:22.100 --> 00:42:25.330
that we already had in place.

00:42:25.330 --> 00:42:28.150
And it's going over the
interconnect, over the peering,

00:42:28.150 --> 00:42:29.210
with Google.

00:42:29.210 --> 00:42:34.620
And then we have one VPN
gateway per region in GCP.

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:37.120
And right now, we
have four regions.

00:42:37.120 --> 00:42:38.800
And then we have a
cloud router in each

00:42:38.800 --> 00:42:40.670
of these regions as well.

00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:42.420
So we pretty much
replicated the setup

00:42:42.420 --> 00:42:45.160
that we already had for
our own data centers.

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:50.320
So we have a full mesh between
our data centers and GCP.

00:42:50.320 --> 00:42:53.500
And it's all going over
peering at this point.

00:42:53.500 --> 00:42:56.020
So as you can see, we
have a different subnet

00:42:56.020 --> 00:42:58.860
for each region in GCP.

00:42:58.860 --> 00:43:01.930
And we are able to advertise
all of our prefixes

00:43:01.930 --> 00:43:04.170
from every single rack into GCP.

00:43:07.780 --> 00:43:12.330
So how we integrate this
into our already existing

00:43:12.330 --> 00:43:15.640
provisioning tool is that we--

00:43:15.640 --> 00:43:19.330
I mean, we had a provisioning
tool, which allowed us

00:43:19.330 --> 00:43:21.820
to abstract, for example,
the DNS generation

00:43:21.820 --> 00:43:24.110
for all the servers.

00:43:24.110 --> 00:43:29.050
So for the developer, they used
to have to go into this tool

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:33.130
and request a
server or multiple.

00:43:33.130 --> 00:43:36.040
And they can simply pick a
platform between bare metal

00:43:36.040 --> 00:43:37.530
or Google Cloud.

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:40.000
And that will take
care of creating

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:43.980
the instances or the VMs, and
also associated to the XPN

00:43:43.980 --> 00:43:47.620
or master project to give
them networking access

00:43:47.620 --> 00:43:52.360
and VPN back to the on-premise
and share all the security

00:43:52.360 --> 00:43:53.370
policies and so on.

00:43:56.170 --> 00:43:57.670
So this is how that tool looks.

00:43:57.670 --> 00:44:03.280
It's called System Zed, or
System Z. And as you can see,

00:44:03.280 --> 00:44:06.520
you can pick Google
Cloud or Bare Metal.

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:10.435
And eventually, the Bare
Metal option will go away.

00:44:10.435 --> 00:44:13.460
And once you press
that, or Google Cloud,

00:44:13.460 --> 00:44:15.900
you get some more options,
like what role it is.

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:19.396
And that's the internal
microservice, for example.

00:44:19.396 --> 00:44:20.920
And if you know
the selectware it's

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:24.730
supposed to be deployed in,
what region, physically,

00:44:24.730 --> 00:44:27.370
and how much CPU and
RAM that you need.

00:44:30.130 --> 00:44:33.550
So how do we feel so
far we've gone to GCP?

00:44:33.550 --> 00:44:37.030
We've had a great
experience so far.

00:44:37.030 --> 00:44:39.050
And from a network
engineer's perspective,

00:44:39.050 --> 00:44:40.740
it's been very stable.

00:44:40.740 --> 00:44:43.150
The IPSec has been great.

00:44:43.150 --> 00:44:46.420
And no matter if
it's IPSec or not,

00:44:46.420 --> 00:44:49.035
the peering has been
very stable as well.

00:44:49.035 --> 00:44:53.290
And the thing is it's not
only about migrating VM for VM

00:44:53.290 --> 00:44:56.770
or going to creating
a container.

00:44:56.770 --> 00:44:59.690
It's also about us using more
and more of the platform tools.

00:44:59.690 --> 00:45:04.180
And yeah, it's a big step.

00:45:04.180 --> 00:45:07.570
And we sometimes notice
in the networking team

00:45:07.570 --> 00:45:10.450
that people are experimenting
with features that we're not

00:45:10.450 --> 00:45:13.090
used to from the network side.

00:45:13.090 --> 00:45:16.050
For example, the Google
Container Engine or Kubernetes.

00:45:16.050 --> 00:45:19.230
And that's a whole
another thing for us

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:22.300
and something that we need
to learn how to handle.

00:45:22.300 --> 00:45:25.450
But luckily, we have
the Google support.

00:45:25.450 --> 00:45:28.630
And that's something that we're
relying pretty heavily on.

00:45:28.630 --> 00:45:30.790
And it's a big mindset change.

00:45:30.790 --> 00:45:33.390
Because before, any
developer could pretty much

00:45:33.390 --> 00:45:36.490
go up to any person face to face
and ask how a system is working

00:45:36.490 --> 00:45:39.620
or how the hardware provisioning
is done, for example,

00:45:39.620 --> 00:45:42.160
but now we're relying
on one support portal.

00:45:44.950 --> 00:45:47.106
So just one more thing.

00:45:47.106 --> 00:45:52.390
This was added today to the
slide, so it's brand new.

00:45:52.390 --> 00:45:56.860
So as we got XPN and we started
putting all the microservices

00:45:56.860 --> 00:46:02.500
in GCP, which was ramped up
pretty heavily in December

00:46:02.500 --> 00:46:07.780
last year, we were actually
able to move all of our users

00:46:07.780 --> 00:46:10.630
away from our San Jose
data center yesterday.

00:46:10.630 --> 00:46:14.570
So in this graph, you can
see that the green line,

00:46:14.570 --> 00:46:17.370
that's GCP US Central.

00:46:17.370 --> 00:46:19.930
The red line is
the GCP Asia East.

00:46:19.930 --> 00:46:23.160
And the blue line is our
own on-premise San Jose data

00:46:23.160 --> 00:46:24.550
center.

00:46:24.550 --> 00:46:28.810
So that means that 28% of all
the traffic, all the users,

00:46:28.810 --> 00:46:32.710
are connected to
GCP at this point.

00:46:32.710 --> 00:46:35.806
And yes, that's it.

00:46:35.806 --> 00:46:38.674
