So I guess, yeah.

00:46:53.730 --> 00:46:55.940
AUDIENCE: So I have a
question for the Google folks.

00:46:55.940 --> 00:46:58.640
Could you expand a little
more on the notion of the host

00:46:58.640 --> 00:46:59.660
project?

00:46:59.660 --> 00:47:04.370
Do you see customers who
have an organization having

00:47:04.370 --> 00:47:07.860
a single host project that
has all the networking for--

00:47:07.860 --> 00:47:10.280
where they manage the networking
for all their projects

00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:10.790
underneath?

00:47:10.790 --> 00:47:12.800
Or do you see related
projects being

00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:15.754
grouped under different hosts
projects under an organization?

00:47:15.754 --> 00:47:16.670
INES ENVID: Thank you.

00:47:16.670 --> 00:47:21.440
Yeah, actually, the
concept of the host project

00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:26.330
is the place where you can
have a shared VPC on XPN.

00:47:26.330 --> 00:47:29.060
It does not mean
that it needs to be

00:47:29.060 --> 00:47:30.780
unique for the organization.

00:47:30.780 --> 00:47:32.610
So some examples.

00:47:32.610 --> 00:47:34.910
We've seen use
cases in which you

00:47:34.910 --> 00:47:38.270
can have a test environment, a
staging environment, production

00:47:38.270 --> 00:47:39.180
environment.

00:47:39.180 --> 00:47:41.600
And those are three
separate networks

00:47:41.600 --> 00:47:43.700
that are managed by
three different teams.

00:47:43.700 --> 00:47:46.540
So in the organization, you
can have more than one host

00:47:46.540 --> 00:47:47.360
project.

00:47:47.360 --> 00:47:51.650
And then each of those
will be a shareable VPC

00:47:51.650 --> 00:47:54.380
that will be administered
by a different team

00:47:54.380 --> 00:47:56.420
for those purposes.

00:47:56.420 --> 00:48:01.540
So you can definitely have sets
of service projects sharing

00:48:01.540 --> 00:48:02.530
a domain.

00:48:02.530 --> 00:48:07.550
And actually, in some
of the other talks,

00:48:07.550 --> 00:48:11.720
you will see that we're as well
starting support VPC peering.

00:48:11.720 --> 00:48:17.000
So the XPNs that
are shareable, there

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:19.760
could be as well as they
need to be peered together.

00:48:19.760 --> 00:48:21.880
So that gives you a
super flexible model

00:48:21.880 --> 00:48:24.290
in having a centralized
shared domain

00:48:24.290 --> 00:48:26.900
and then peer as you
need with others.

00:48:26.900 --> 00:48:28.670
So that's kind of
the vision that we're

00:48:28.670 --> 00:48:30.368
having for flexible topologies.

00:48:36.725 --> 00:48:40.120
AUDIENCE: Would this allow me
to set up a BigQuery instance

00:48:40.120 --> 00:48:43.560
that I can only connect
to from on-premise and not

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:44.890
through the internet?

00:48:44.890 --> 00:48:45.640
INES ENVID: Right.

00:48:45.640 --> 00:48:47.500
Yeah, great question.

00:48:47.500 --> 00:48:51.280
Those are some of the
things that we're actually

00:48:51.280 --> 00:48:53.170
looking at right now.

00:48:53.170 --> 00:48:58.630
We do have a very strong
vision on providing

00:48:58.630 --> 00:49:01.060
private connectivity,
extending all the capabilities

00:49:01.060 --> 00:49:03.820
that we have today from
our VMs to anything

00:49:03.820 --> 00:49:05.980
that goes on-premises.

00:49:05.980 --> 00:49:10.825
So we are working on some
of those capabilities

00:49:10.825 --> 00:49:14.830
to actually extend that
private access to on-premises.

00:49:14.830 --> 00:49:19.000
And some of those things will
keep coming up in the roadmap.

00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:21.980
We're not ready to
announce at this point.

00:49:21.980 --> 00:49:24.500
But this is really extending
all those capabilities

00:49:24.500 --> 00:49:28.510
to on-premise is
definitely in our kind

00:49:28.510 --> 00:49:33.124
of short, medium-term view.

00:49:33.124 --> 00:49:35.040
NEHA PATTAN: It would
be good to hear from you

00:49:35.040 --> 00:49:37.280
what the requirements are
specifically about this.

00:49:37.280 --> 00:49:41.880
So later after this talk, maybe
we can chat about what exactly

00:49:41.880 --> 00:49:42.750
you're looking for.

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:45.690
INES ENVID: Yeah, we're, by
the way, meeting in the Meet

00:49:45.690 --> 00:49:49.710
the PM area that is on
the third floor close

00:49:49.710 --> 00:49:52.700
to the room for the
keynote, I believe.

00:49:52.700 --> 00:49:55.200
I think it's closer
to the security booth.

00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:59.156
So we can actually go in
depth about those use cases.

00:49:59.156 --> 00:50:00.898
AUDIENCE: Excuse me.

