00:51:18.020 --> 00:51:22.106
to everything that is API
[INAUDIBLE] it's BigQuery,

00:51:22.106 --> 00:51:25.850
BigTable, PubSub, Stackdriver.

00:51:25.850 --> 00:51:30.059
All that is beta at this point.

00:51:30.059 --> 00:51:30.600
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:51:30.600 --> 00:51:31.433
This is [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:31.433 --> 00:51:33.700
I have a question
on performance,

00:51:33.700 --> 00:51:35.830
especially for the Spotify.

00:51:35.830 --> 00:51:38.872
So when you moved from
the project-centric

00:51:38.872 --> 00:51:42.540
and the networking model to
much more at the organization

00:51:42.540 --> 00:51:46.390
level, right, the [? shared ?]
Cross-Project Networking,

00:51:46.390 --> 00:51:49.275
do you see any
performance improvements?

00:51:49.275 --> 00:51:51.780
And can he just talk about
general observations?

00:51:54.430 --> 00:51:56.460
INES ENVID: Yeah,
another great question.

00:51:56.460 --> 00:51:59.050
There is absolutely
no performance impact.

00:51:59.050 --> 00:52:02.670
So you won't notice
any difference at all.

00:52:02.670 --> 00:52:05.284
So everything will work
pretty much as everything

00:52:05.284 --> 00:52:06.450
was in the same [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:06.450 --> 00:52:08.940
The reason is very
simple for us.

00:52:08.940 --> 00:52:14.310
Really, as a networking
domain, our data plan,

00:52:14.310 --> 00:52:17.520
it's really a program
as in the domain

00:52:17.520 --> 00:52:21.180
of where the VPC is defined.

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:25.380
So meaning that
every VM that gets

00:52:25.380 --> 00:52:28.940
created as part of that private
domain, it is a single--

00:52:28.940 --> 00:52:32.400
it is completely program across.

00:52:32.400 --> 00:52:34.506
So it does not really
matter whether they

00:52:34.506 --> 00:52:35.797
belong to the same [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:35.797 --> 00:52:37.547
That's more like an
administrative control

00:52:37.547 --> 00:52:40.590
[INAUDIBLE] construct
that we don't really are

00:52:40.590 --> 00:52:43.029
even aware of when we
actually [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:43.029 --> 00:52:44.320
AUDIENCE: Would that be a gain?

00:52:44.320 --> 00:52:46.300
I mean, it's a minor
gain, [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:46.300 --> 00:52:50.350
Because you don't have to
jump across multiple VPCs,

00:52:50.350 --> 00:52:50.850
for example.

00:52:50.850 --> 00:52:56.070
[INAUDIBLE] microservices, now
I can have 800 projects all

00:52:56.070 --> 00:52:56.905
in a single--

00:52:56.905 --> 00:52:59.462
potentially a single
networking pipe.

00:52:59.462 --> 00:53:00.960
So would that be
a potential gain?

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:03.270
INES ENVID: Yeah, it is
definitely a potential gain as

00:53:03.270 --> 00:53:08.670
compared to how you will
actually build that topology

00:53:08.670 --> 00:53:10.020
without having XPN.

00:53:10.020 --> 00:53:13.120
That is definitely
an improvement.

00:53:13.120 --> 00:53:17.030
So again, the performance,
it will be exactly the same

00:53:17.030 --> 00:53:20.506
as if you had a single
project with a single VPC,

00:53:20.506 --> 00:53:22.755
even though you have all
these different projects that

00:53:22.755 --> 00:53:24.210
are actually sharing it.

00:53:24.210 --> 00:53:28.030
So yeah, as compared to
traditional topologies,

00:53:28.030 --> 00:53:30.226
we're seeing, definitely,
a big win there.

00:53:38.830 --> 00:53:40.010
Any other questions?

00:53:40.010 --> 00:53:42.040
Have one for Stefan.

00:53:42.040 --> 00:53:44.360
We've been working so hard
for the last [INAUDIBLE].

00:53:44.360 --> 00:53:45.580
NEHA PATTAN: I thought the
network performance question

00:53:45.580 --> 00:53:46.880
was actually for Stefan.

00:53:46.880 --> 00:53:48.795
Did you want to mention
anything about--

00:53:48.795 --> 00:53:50.722
INES ENVID: Actually,
that's correct.

00:53:50.722 --> 00:53:52.674
It's better that you
answer the performance.

00:53:52.674 --> 00:53:53.840
What's been your experience?

00:53:53.840 --> 00:53:57.430
STEFAN LINDBLOM:
Yeah, so every tunnel,

00:53:57.430 --> 00:53:58.990
[INAUDIBLE] every
IPSec tunnel, is

00:53:58.990 --> 00:54:03.400
limited to about 3
gigabits of IPSec capacity.

00:54:03.400 --> 00:54:08.140
And for us, if we need more,
we just add more tunnels.

00:54:08.140 --> 00:54:09.670
So we actually
have, for example,

00:54:09.670 --> 00:54:13.750
a script that we use
to generate tunnels.

00:54:13.750 --> 00:54:17.500
So we don't try to manage
each tunnel on its own.

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:22.770
INES ENVID: Can you talk a
little bit about, you know,

00:54:22.770 --> 00:54:25.290
to the question of the
gentleman about what's

00:54:25.290 --> 00:54:28.400
been your experience of the
performance of Cross-Project

00:54:28.400 --> 00:54:32.870
Networking as compared
to traditional VPC?

00:54:32.870 --> 00:54:33.786
STEFAN LINDBLOM: Yeah.

00:54:33.786 --> 00:54:39.420
Yeah, so we started with having
no VPC or the traditional way.

00:54:39.420 --> 00:54:45.120
And yeah, it's a
lot harder to scale.

00:54:45.120 --> 00:54:47.220
Because if you have
a lot of projects,

00:54:47.220 --> 00:54:50.250
then you need to add VPN
tunnels for each project.

00:54:50.250 --> 00:54:53.580
And that took a lot of
time from the network team.

00:54:53.580 --> 00:54:55.050
Even if we have a
script to do it,

00:54:55.050 --> 00:54:57.850
we need to run it for
every individual project.

00:54:57.850 --> 00:55:01.090
And also care about
IP ranges and so on.

00:55:01.090 --> 00:55:05.860
But with XPN, we're
having a shared network.

00:55:05.860 --> 00:55:06.660
It's a lot easier.

00:55:06.660 --> 00:55:08.520
We just create the
tunnels in the same place

00:55:08.520 --> 00:55:13.260
and we automatically
get those capabilities

00:55:13.260 --> 00:55:18.040
for all the associated
projects in XPN.

00:55:18.040 --> 00:55:20.960
INES ENVID: And you see no
impact in the performance?

00:55:20.960 --> 00:55:23.630
STEFAN LINDBLOM: No, there's
definitely no negative impact

00:55:23.630 --> 00:55:25.880
by doing that.

00:55:25.880 --> 00:55:27.890
Yeah, and as I said, we
just add more tunnels

00:55:27.890 --> 00:55:29.600
if we need more performance.

00:55:29.600 --> 00:55:33.164
So it scales linearly.

00:55:33.164 --> 00:55:34.580
NEHA PATTAN: Yeah,
the performance

00:55:34.580 --> 00:55:39.260
would be pretty much the same
as a single project or the same

00:55:39.260 --> 00:55:42.380
as if you had peering
between the two projects.

00:55:42.380 --> 00:55:44.000
But basically,
the gains that you

00:55:44.000 --> 00:55:47.140
get from a shared
VPC, that is XPN,

00:55:47.140 --> 00:55:50.810
is mostly in simplifying
the management.

00:55:50.810 --> 00:55:53.010
So basically, there's
no loss in performance.

00:55:53.010 --> 00:55:55.670
It's pretty much the same
optimized performance.

00:55:55.670 --> 00:55:57.950
But you also gain by
not having to spend

00:55:57.950 --> 00:56:00.140
on the extra management
that is involved

00:56:00.140 --> 00:56:05.170
with having separate side load
projects and separate networks.

00:56:05.170 --> 00:56:07.460
There's one more question?

00:56:07.460 --> 00:56:08.260
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:56:08.260 --> 00:56:11.664
I didn't see anything
about IP version 6.

00:56:11.664 --> 00:56:12.580
Does it support IP v6?

00:56:18.260 --> 00:56:20.640
INES ENVID: The answer is no.

00:56:20.640 --> 00:56:25.550
We are looking
very seriously now

00:56:25.550 --> 00:56:31.210
at building the IP
v6 capabilities.

00:56:31.210 --> 00:56:33.900
And this is pretty much
on top of our list.

00:56:33.900 --> 00:56:38.490
So I think you'll see a
lot of improvements there.

00:56:38.490 --> 00:56:45.530
We do have certain capabilities,
which are in alpha.

00:56:45.530 --> 00:56:48.530
So when I'm talking
about the support,

00:56:48.530 --> 00:56:52.280
I'm mainly talking about
what is available in beta.

00:56:52.280 --> 00:56:55.270
But we do have capabilities
in alpha today.

00:56:55.270 --> 00:57:01.024
And as we progress, you will
see a lot more coming soon.

00:57:01.024 --> 00:57:02.940
NEHA PATTAN: So just to
clarify on that point,

00:57:02.940 --> 00:57:05.690
VMs cannot have v6 IPs.

00:57:05.690 --> 00:57:07.870
We just recently
announced the alpha

00:57:07.870 --> 00:57:13.210
